#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074863} :activity/id #uuid "37e46b78-af93-563c-9c22-49fec2ac17ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050357 :topic/id #uuid "4226fd5b-b1b5-57e0-a1c2-d40bfd3ac949" :topic/math-academy-id 521} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074976 :tutorial/content "Two intersecting lines are **perpendicular** if they form a right angle (an angle that measures $90^\\circ$). \n\n![](images/ee/ee6258a8a148ae3a8f4ce63321bf9f4dfa955eb0a90b332477e6cc933cad7b74.png)\n\nFor example, the two lines above are perpendicular because the angle $\\angle BOD$ is a right angle.\n\nThe opposite is also true: if two lines are perpendicular, then they meet at a right angle.\n\nAs you may have noticed already, when two lines are perpendicular, they don't form only one right angle. All the four angles formed by the intersection are right angles!\n\n![](images/15/152aa56c4fd669e417cb20f1bef8fcec7e7ea34e0be9e44e7acad3ac490e5713.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ffd3b7d9-a64e-5147-bd6d-d2892a17bbb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2185 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "30171190-d63a-5632-937f-c723296dc4a5" :step/math-academy-id 73809 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074864}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075003 :tutorial/content "A segment **bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that splits another segment into two congruent parts.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is a bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}.$\n\n![](images/1c/1cf8d2956dd054509cb421a1b706063ea318f14d0575295634baf0fc5679105c.png)\n\nA **perpendicular bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that bisects another segment *and is perpendicular to that segment*.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{UV}$ is a perpendicular bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}.$\n\n![](images/01/01cf976113ad5c2f635548aab38434c4add38c61195aacc96e611077aa4df606.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4e7bceb-cc96-58c7-b89d-38f59696caaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3122 :tutorial/title "Bisectors"} :step/id #uuid "ea487a60-e3d3-53f8-8fe6-f94b5665c98a" :step/math-academy-id 73810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074865}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073584 :question/id #uuid "d49eb5a2-baaf-527f-bfa7-12b2befa865a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2609"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fbb6558-01d5-5d08-8e84-64077353b2a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050476}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492876}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Perpendicular Bisectors"} :step/id #uuid "a66c9782-dc26-51cf-a396-ca74eae155be" :step/math-academy-id 73811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074987 :tutorial/content "The **perpendicular bisector  theorem** states that:\n\nIn a plane, if a point is on the perpendicular bisector of a segment, then it is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b45782d21e6ffdff1fd46956b1933d976df90e69f8d5e15e2edf116fe11c0e8e.png)\n\n Consequently, we can conclude that the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ So we have $AD=BD.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eefe034d-1e7e-55b2-9f1c-58f2df60defc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2360 :tutorial/title "The Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "c0f78ca3-27f5-5550-9f9e-4a6e83a3019a" :step/math-academy-id 73814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073585 :question/id #uuid "01b53ce1-80d9-530a-bfba-cfd500b37f9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cda2e87-1a65-5075-96fd-98a8a4fd7d7a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492873}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "e41c50b6-9fa3-57d8-8d33-d797739dc3d7" :step/math-academy-id 73815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074988 :tutorial/content "The **converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem** states that:\n\nIn a plane, if a point is equidistant from the endpoints of a segment, then it is on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{AB},$ and the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\n![](images/d1/d17f25984a6e153a6f155552e7da4a0d67241ad7e83eabefb5ad5b663827fbce.png)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ In particular, we conclude that $AC = BC.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2224f56e-4101-55a0-9dd0-05903bc3226b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2361 :tutorial/title "The Converse of the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3eb6ca80-41aa-5855-a721-79268fd23a3a" :step/math-academy-id 73818 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073586 :question/id #uuid "ab4fe8cf-3651-5a1f-a796-546892cb0178" :question/math-academy-id "e-2613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e57b78-5159-51bc-aeaf-9530f641262b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492914}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Converse of the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "5f561bba-2e4e-56f5-b6aa-667b06db1509" :step/math-academy-id 73819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075002 :tutorial/content "The most important property of perpendicular lines is the following.\n\nGiven a line $l$ and a point $P$, there is one and only one line $m$ that passes through $P$ and is perpendicular to $l$.\n\n![](images/24/24e78b2f90aa4cfd9a6eba5ace46195536377c19d255e51e82485af69c866292.png)\n\nOther nice properties include the following:\n\n- If two distinct lines are perpendicular to the same (third) line, then these two lines are parallel. For example, in the figure below, both lines $n$ and $m$ are perpendicular to the line $l$. As a result, $n \\parallel m$.\n\n- If a line is perpendicular to one of two parallel lines, then that line is also perpendicular to the second parallel line.\n\n![](images/64/64bae80f45df4e2536e3c929471352e9d911607efeeab9bd835acebf4ff0af01.png)\n\nSymbol $\\perp$\n\nFinally, if a line $p$ is perpendicular to a line $q$, we can denote it without words using the symbol $\\perp$ (perpendicular), as follows:\n\n$$\np \\perp q\n$$\n\nThis reads as \"*$p$ is perpendicular to $q$*.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "b323a149-5fd9-529b-8eb0-28a8cd06e56c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2985 :tutorial/title "Basic Properties of Perpendicular Lines"} :step/id #uuid "f9beea6b-dcdf-5676-8112-d86aa617b006" :step/math-academy-id 73822}] :activity/title "Segment Bisectors and the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073943}]}
